[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: spine_router_0_0
diagnosis_text:
The affected component is the BGP control-plane on spine_router_0_0: the bgpd daemon is not running. This explains why all BGP sessions to that spine (from super_spine_router_0 and both leaf routers) dropped within seconds; with no bgpd to accept TCP/179 sessions, peers remain in Active and their hold timers expire simultaneously. Data-plane links and interfaces are fine, so traffic continues to flow via the remaining spine, preserving host-to-host reachability but removing redundancy.

Localization: on spine_router_0_0, frr vtysh reports “bgpd is not running” for show ip bgp summary, systemd shows frr.service inactive (dead), and ps/netstat show no bgpd process nor TCP/2605 listener. Yet interfaces eth0/eth1/eth2 are UP with link detected and correct /31 addressing, matching the observation that “all interfaces show UP.” On peers, super_spine_router_0 and both leaf routers show their neighbor toward spine_router_0_0 stuck in Active while their sessions to spine_router_0_1 are Established, consistent with a single-spine control-plane failure.

Root cause: the bgpd process on spine_router_0_0 stopped (crashed or was stopped), causing all BGP adjacencies on that node to drop at once.

Corrective fix: restart FRR/bgpd on spine_router_0_0 (e.g., systemctl restart frr) and verify show ip bgp summary returns Established to all neighbors. Then review /var/log/frr/frr.log and journalctl for crash/assert messages, ensure bgpd is enabled in /etc/frr/daemons (it is), and re-enable/monitor the frr service so it remains active. Consider enabling watchfrr or a process supervisor for automatic restart on failure. 
=== END ===